From c8066479620488cd48bef37842d3c50cd57c7958 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 13:18:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] raster: band-parallel scanline fills in the managed
 rasterizer

Multithreading roadmap item #4. Every fill in BCanvas is a scanline loop whose
rows write disjoint pixels and read state that is settled before the loop starts,
so the y range is split into bands across threads and nothing else changes.

BRasterParallelism owns the budget (BROILER_RASTER_THREADS, default one thread
per core) and the split decision. A fill below the area threshold runs inline on
exactly the code path it took before, which is the common case: a page of text is
thousands of glyph fills of a few hundred pixels each.

The threshold is measured rather than assumed. At the first value tried, 24K
pixels, not one fill on four of the five corpus pages reached it and the feature
was inert; the corpus renders 2,200-pixel fills in bulk and hundred-thousand-pixel
fills almost never. At 2,048 the paint-heavy page splits 92.7% of its fill area
and renders 1.42x faster at four threads, pixels identical.

FillGlyphContours' coverage accumulator and crossing list move inside the band,
which is the only per-fill mutable state the rasterizer had.

Band parallelism is gated on BBitmap.SupportsConcurrentPixelWrites: a surface
that has materialized a platform bitmap keeps the single-threaded rasterizer it
was written against, since its threading rules are not ours to assume.
